findings.description:
HEREDOC = re.compile(r"<<-?\s*(['"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1[^
]*
(?:.*?
)*?\s*\2[ 	]*(?=
|$)", re.S) is compiled with re.S, so `.` inside `(?:.*?
)*?` also matches newlines. Each iteration of the group can end at any later newline, so for a command containing `<<WORD` with N following newlines and no matching terminator line, the engine explores on the order of 2^N segmentations before failing. Python's re has no backtracking limit. The input is the agent's Bash command text; `awk '{ x = a<<b }'`, a `<<EOF` whose terminator is followed by `;` or `)`, or an unterminated heredoc in a 30-plus line script will make strip_heredocs hang the grade for minutes or hours.

findings.suggested_fix:
Drop re.S and match line-wise: `<<-?\s*(['"]?)(\w+)\1[^
]*
(?:[^
]*
)*?[ 	]*\2[ 	]*(?=
|$)` (each iteration consumes exactly one line, so backtracking is linear), or parse heredocs with a simple line scanner instead of a regex. Add a unit test with an unterminated 40-line heredoc and a time bound.
